[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"HTTP Module: Creating HTTP Servers and Clients in Node.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Node.js provides a built-in HTTP module that allows you to create HTTP servers and clients with ease. This module is fundamental for building web applications, APIs, and network services. This tutorial will guide you through the process of creating both HTTP servers and clients using the 'http' module."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an HTTP Server"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create an HTTP server, you need to require the 'http' module and use the 'http.createServer()' method. This method takes a request listener function as an argument, which is executed every time a new request is received by the server."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
  res.end('Hello, World!\n');
});

const port = 3000;
server.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}/`);
});"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the code above:
- 'require('http')' imports the HTTP module.
- 'http.createServer()' creates a new HTTP server.
- The request listener function '(req, res) => { ... }' handles incoming requests and sends responses.
- 'res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' })' sets the response status code to 200 (OK) and the content type to plain text.
- 'res.end('Hello, World!\n')' sends the response body and closes the connection.
- 'server.listen(port, () => { ... })' starts the server and listens for connections on the specified port."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Different Routes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use the 'req.url' property to determine the requested URL and handle different routes accordingly."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === '/') {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Welcome to the homepage!\n');
  } else if (req.url === '/about') {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('About us page\n');
  } else {
    res.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('404 Not Found\n');
  }
});

const port = 3000;
server.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${port}/`);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates how to handle different routes based on the 'req.url' property. If the URL is '/', it responds with 'Welcome to the homepage!'. If the URL is '/about', it responds with 'About us page'. Otherwise, it responds with a 404 Not Found error."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an HTTP Client"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create an HTTP client, you can use the 'http.request()' or 'http.get()' methods. 'http.request()' provides more control over the request, while 'http.get()' is a shorthand for simple GET requests."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const http = require('http');

const options = {
  hostname: 'www.example.com',
  port: 80,
  path: '/',
  method: 'GET'
};

const req = http.request(options, (res) => {
  console.log(`Status: ${res.statusCode}`);
  console.log(`Headers: ${JSON.stringify(res.headers)}`);
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log(`Body: ${chunk}`);
  });
  res.on('end', () => {
    console.log('No more data in response.');
  });
});

req.on('error', (e) => {
  console.error(`Problem with request: ${e.message}`);
});

req.end();"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the code above:
- 'options' is an object that specifies the details of the request, such as the hostname, port, path, and method.
- 'http.request(options, (res) => { ... })' creates a new HTTP request. The callback function is executed when the response is received.
- 'res.statusCode' contains the HTTP status code of the response.
- 'res.headers' contains the HTTP headers of the response.
- 'res.setEncoding('utf8')' sets the encoding of the response body to UTF-8.
- 'res.on('data', (chunk) => { ... })' is an event listener that is called whenever a new chunk of data is received.
- 'res.on('end', () => { ... })' is an event listener that is called when the entire response has been received.
- 'req.on('error', (e) => { ... })' is an event listener that is called if there is an error with the request.
- 'req.end()' sends the request."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Using http.get()"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"'http.get()' simplifies making GET requests. Here's an example:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const http = require('http');

http.get('http://www.example.com', (res) => {
  console.log(`Status: ${res.statusCode}`);
  console.log(`Headers: ${JSON.stringify(res.headers)}`);
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log(`Body: ${chunk}`);
  });
  res.on('end', () => {
    console.log('No more data in response.');
  });
}).on('error', (e) => {
  console.error(`Got error: ${e.message}`);
});"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code achieves the same result as the previous example but with less code. 'http.get()' automatically sends the request."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling POST Requests"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"To send POST requests, you need to set the 'method' option to 'POST' and write the data to the request body."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const http = require('http');

const postData = JSON.stringify({
  'msg': 'Hello World!'
});

const options = {
  hostname: 'www.example.com',
  port: 80,
  path: '/submit',
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'Content-Length': Buffer.byteLength(postData)
  }
};

const req = http.request(options, (res) => {
  console.log(`Status: ${res.statusCode}`);
  console.log(`Headers: ${JSON.stringify(res.headers)}`);
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log(`Body: ${chunk}`);
  });
  res.on('end', () => {
    console.log('No more data in response.');
  });
});

req.on('error', (e) => {
  console.error(`Problem with request: ${e.message}`);
});

req.write(postData);
req.end();"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:
- 'postData' is the data that will be sent in the request body.
- The 'Content-Type' header is set to 'application/json' to indicate that the data is in JSON format.
- The 'Content-Length' header is set to the length of the data in bytes.
- 'req.write(postData)' writes the data to the request body.
- 'req.end()' sends the request."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of creating HTTP servers and clients using the Node.js 'http' module. You can further explore the module's documentation for more advanced features and options."}]
